I'm Evan Carmichael welcome to another  edition of ask Evan today we're  continuing our challenge series with  Telus talks business where we're  answering questions from you guys who  have a business and have questions  today's question comes from one of our  Facebook friends from Telus talks  businesses in wins Jeremy and Jamie  wrote in great advice to Mark's question  in my business mentor network I just  launched a pilot of a new platform  called mentor up how do I know when my  new product is validated to the point  where I'm ready to move out of the pilot  beta phase and start scaling so great  question Jeremy thanks for asking it I'm  going to give you three suggestions that  I hope will help you  number one get past survival the first  challenge for entrepreneurs in start-up  mode is getting past the survival stage  you need to find a way to bringing  enough money to pay your bills and put  food on the table you don't have to make  a ton of money yet that may be the  eventual goal but it's not what you need  right now most businesses fail and a big  reason is because entrepreneurs run out  of money too quickly it's great to have  big ambitions to be optimistic about the  future that's what we do as  entrepreneurs but you have to first find  a way to get to the point where you know  you're making enough money so that you  don't have to worry about going to get a  job in a few months now Jeremy I don't  know where you are at financial with  your business at the moment but if  you're not pass the survival phase worry  about that before thinking about scaling  invest your time and not your money into  making the model work and bringing  enough dollars in to support yourself  and your family get close to your  customers provide outstanding service  change your model as many times as you  need to in order to solve their problems  and you're on your way to break in  through number to make one dollar bring  in more than one dollar once you're past  your survival phase it's time to start  investing some money into your business  in the early days of any business I'm a  big believer in bootstrapping only  invest money that you have from your  business and put it back into your  business you know from the survival  phase that you can turn your time into  money now you've got to turn money into  more money you have a model that works  on a small scale and it's paying the  bills where can you invest some of your  dollars into growing the business is it  building a website investing in  advertising hiring a part-time employee  doing trade shows running contests try a  few different ideas that you think will  have a high payout and start on a really  small scale invest minimal dollars and  see if it generates a return keep  playing with the model until you find a  way to turn every one dollar of  investment into more than one dollar in  return you'll find that some things work  well and others don't maybe trade shows  bringing a lot of business but postcard  advertising dozen don't invest big  dollars in anything until you've shown  that it can work on a small scale you  may get frustrated with the early  results but it's important to stick with  it you're building a model for rapid  growth and you need to know which levers  before you can scale number three use  proof of concept to scale if you  followed steps one and two you now have  a business that feeds you step one and  you know how to get more growth by  investing your dollar strategically step  two now it's time to ramp up how big you  grow will depend on how big your  ambition is if you can turn one dollar  into more than one dollar in return then  you can either build slowly and continue  your growth organically or you can raise  capital to expand more quickly even  though I was in the venture capital  business I'm generally a fan of the  organic growth you make fewer mistakes  and build a more solid business just  keep plowing your profits back into the  systems that you know generate returns  along the way you have to think about  staffing and all the issues that come  with a growing business and I'm of the  belief that it's better to tackle those  things one at a time you're more likely  to get it right if you want to go more  quickly though this is the time to do it  since you know how to turn money into  more money it's easier and much less  costly they get financial help you  during the form of a loan or equity  investor before you pass through steps  one and two your business is risky for  anybody to invest money with because  it's riskier they'll want higher  interest rates or bigger percentage of  your business once you get to step three  your business is more solid and it's  easier to get favorable terms from  people how much money you need and when  you need it depends on how quickly you  want to grow thank you for joining me  for another edition of ask Evan if you  like the video you want to see more  please give it a thumbs up below it  makes you want to do more these videos  for you guys I'd also love to hear what  you have to think if you want to leave a  comment under the video or if you have a  question for your own business maybe  we'll feature you in one of the upcoming  challenge video series so thank you and  you'll see you on the next episode  you  you
 

